LAB2 is not an issue nowadays as that's an exam and it doesn't contain the 
diagnostic test as it used too.
So we will not send reminders about that, but we will send reminders about the 
other three modules.
Those tree modules can also be finished at any time, unlike LAB2 that must be 
taken when the exam is given.

Here we provide a script that will email students at suitable points in time 
and intervals.

\subsection{The script}

The script is a bash script.
Cron will execute the script at suitable points in time.
We also want to be able to source it for debugging and use in other scripts.
<<[[missing.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash

<<constants>>
<<functions>>

# Run the script if not sourced
if [[ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" == "${0}" ]]; then
  main
fi
@

\subsection{When to run the script}

The course is given during autumns, September to mid-January.
The different modules have different timelines.

KAL1 should be done by October.
The labs can usually be presented at most lab sessions during the course.
After the course ends it can only be presented during Lab Week in June.

LAB1 should be done by November.
These labs can also be presented at any lab session during the course.
After the course ends they can be presented at Restlabb every week during the 
semester, but also during Lab Week in June.

LAB3 should be done by mid-January.
After the course ends they can present these assignments at Restlabb every week 
during the semester, but also during Lab Week in June.
The diagnostic test can be done at any time as it requires no grading.

The script should optimally be run around the time the students think that they 
are done with a module.
Then I want a reminder at the end of every month.
(This ought to be researched.
Particularly, it should be founded in the nudging research literature.)

Cron will run the script at the end of every month.
So the [[main]] function must look at the current month and determine which 
modules to check for.

\subsection{The email}

We get the students' email addresses as the third field of the output of 
[[canvaslms results --missing]].
(The field that we [[cut]] away above.)
We want to send one email per student.
We can use the [[mutt]] command to send the email.

We want the subject to contain the course (column 1) and something indicating 
that this is an automatic reminder about things left to do to get a grade.

The body of the email should contain a list of the missing assignments and the 
motivations for why they are missing.
It should also contain a brief description of how to proceed.
For example, for the missing diagnostic tests, the email should say that they 
should just do it and then they'll get the grade.
But for other assignments, they might need to present during a lab session or 
at Restlabb, then this should be mentioned in the body of the email.

